; expect 42
; -5 against a digit is a negative literal, as stage0 lexes it, not an
; identifier emitted as the SSA name %-5.
;
; And it is a negative literal in ARGUMENT position too, which it was
; not. stage0's lexer emits `Minus` then `IntLiteral(5)` deliberately -
; a negative literal is a parser construct, not a lexical one - but the
; parser only fused the two outside a parenthesised form. So `(+ x -5)`
; was `+` applied to THREE things, silently, and since `+` takes two it
; surfaced as `AX3013 partial application` pointing at the `-`, on a
; program stage1 accepts and runs. Adjacency is the rule now: the two
; tokens have to touch.
;
; `(- 5)` with a space is still negation applied to a positive literal,
; which is what keeps `(- x)` meaning what it always did. Both spellings
; are below, because the fix is only correct if it distinguishes them.
(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (let ((x -5)            ; binding position: was already a literal
        (y (+ 3 -1))      ; argument position: was three arguments
        (z (- 0 -4))      ; and after a leading operand
        (w (- 5)))        ; a space, so still unary minus: -5
    (+ x (+ y (+ z (+ w 46))))))
